        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "The geoglyphs were made by removing the reddish iron-oxide-coated pebbles that cover the desert to expose the pale subsoil, and the lines are typically 10 to 15 centimetres deep.",
        "segment_index": 1,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "81% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 2 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Nazca lines were created between about 500 BC and AD 500 and survive because the local climate is dry and almost windless, with lime-rich subsoil that hardens in the morning mist.",
        "segment_index": 2,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "78% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 1 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Gerald Hawkins and Anthony Aveni concluded in 1990 that the evidence for astronomical alignments was insufficient, and current readings treat the lines as ritual, including sacred pathways and figures invoking water.",
